{"type":"message","id":"caa68777","parentId":"f9792d54","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:19:23.579Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-84d1a8a23e285525","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Shared workspace: Claude, Codex, Qwen\n\nLocal coordination for archeus v1. Excluded from git through `.git/info/exclude`, never committed.\n\n- `BOARD.md` is the task board. One card per unit of work. It is the only queue.\n- `STATUS-<date>.md` is the dated state of play: what has actually been run, what is known, what waits on whom. Read it before re-deriving anything.\n- Analysis notes (a card's argument, kept out of the card's `notes:` when too long for it): `T-010-creator-access.md`, `T-004-evidence.md`.\n- `reviews/T-NNN-<sha7>.md` holds Codex's verdict on a card �?" one file per reviewed commit, which is how `review.py` names them (`out_path` in `review.py`). A re-review after a new commit is a new file, not an edit.\n- `review.py` runs a headless Codex review of a card and writes that file.\n\n## Roles\n\n| Agent | Runs as | Does | Never |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Owner** (Babar) | �?" | Authorizes cards, routes blocked cards, approves pushes | �?" |\n| **Claude** | Claude Code in `D:\\Claude` | Implements most cards: code, tests, local commits, card upkeep | Push; reassign a card; run Windows isolation/profile/ACL APIs without that exact authorization on the card |\n| **Codex** | `codex exec -s read-only` via `review.py` | Architecture-level, read-only review of each card's commits against the canonical docs | Edit files, run tests, commit |\n| **Qwen** (qwen3.8-flash-next on Spark, through `pi`) | `pi --model spark/qwen3.8-flash-next` | Cards the owner assigns to it, usually ones Claude reported as blocked | Push; take a card not assigned to it; anything outside the card's scope |\n\nThe roles in the root `AGENTS.md` (Codex reviews, does not implement) still hold. This file adds Qwen and the board.\n\n## Card lifecycle\n\n```\nproposed �"?�"?owner auth�"?�"?�-� ready �"?�"?�-� doing �"?�"?commit�"?�"?�-� review �"?�"?Codex�"?�"?�-� done\n                                      �"'                 �"'\n                                      �-�                 �""�"?�"?�-� changes �"?�"?�-� doing\n                    BLOCKED BY CLASSIFIER �"?�"?owner routes�"?�"?�-� ready (owner: claude | qwen)\n                    BLOCKED BY OWNER      �"?�"?owner decides�"?�"?�-� ready | proposed\n```\n\n- **proposed**: written by anyone; no code until the owner fills `auth:`.\n- **ready**: `auth:` holds the owner's words and date. A historical approval in another document is not authorization for a card.\n- **doing**: the implementer sets this and lists every file it will touch in `scope:`. No two `doing` cards share a file.\n- **review**: the implementer committed, listed the SHAs in `commits:`, ran the tests named in `verify:` and recorded the result.\n- **changes**: Codex's verdict was CHANGES. The implementer addresses each finding, commits, and returns the card to `review`.\n- **done**: Codex APPROVED, or the owner accepted it explicitly. ESCALATE goes to the owner, never back to the implementer alone.\n- **BLOCKED BY CLASSIFIER** / **BLOCKED BY OWNER**: see below.\n\n## When an agent is blocked\n\nTwo kinds of block, and the status says which one, word for word:\n\n- **`BLOCKED BY CLASSIFIER`**: a safety classifier or a model refusal stopped the agent: Claude Code's auto-mode classifier denying a tool call, a response withheld by a safety classifier, Codex refusing. These are the cards the owner may route to Qwen.\n- **`BLOCKED BY OWNER`**: the card needs something only the owner gives: an authorization that names the operation, a decision, a review the owner must commission. Say which in the first words of `notes:`.\n\n1. Set `status:` to one of the two, exactly as written above.\n2. Under `notes:` paste the exact block message (for a classifier stop, verbatim, including its reason tag), what is done, and what remains, with file and function names.\n3. Stop work on that card and continue with the next `ready` card.\n\nOnly the owner moves a blocked card. Routing it to Qwen means setting `owner: qwen` and status `ready`. No agent reassigns a card to another agent on its own.\n\n**A block label never carries authorization.** `BLOCKED BY CLASSIFIER` says who may take the card; it says nothing about what the card may run. A card that needs a Windows isolation, profile or ACL operation needs an `auth:` naming it whoever holds it �?" moving a card to Qwen does not supply that, and a classifier label must not become the route by which an ACL write reaches a tree archeus does not own.\n\n## Hard rules (every agent, every card)\n\nThese come from `AGENTS.md`, `docs/project-context/archeus-chatgpt.md` and incidents already paid for.\n\n- **Git.** Stage files by name (`git add <path>`), never `git add -A` or `git add .`. The tree holds uncommitted work that belongs to no card. No push, no `git clean`, no `reset --hard`, no `checkout -- <file>`, no branch switch.\n- **Windows isolation, profile and ACL APIs** (AppContainer create/delete, `icacls`, `SetNamedSecurityInfo`, job objects on real processes) run only when the card's `auth:` names that operation. Writing code that calls them is a different permission from executing it.\n- **Never grant or change ACLs on a tree archeus does not own.** S1 removed every grant path (`grant()`, `require_core_owned`, `_call_icacls`; a test asserts they stay gone), and the static gate in `test_pv20_isolation.py` fails on any ACL-writing API in `archeus/`, `claude_sessions/` or `tools/`. ACL code is S2-G's, behind its own authorization. Never point a probe at the Python install, the user profile or a repo root: an ungated grant there blacked out the archeus GUI once already.\n- **Production switches stay off** unless the card says otherwise: `PRODUCTION_DISPATCH_ENABLED`, `A4_ENFORCED`, `CAPABILITIES` in `archeus/node/isolation.py`.\n- **Sign your own work, and take only your own cards.** An artifact's byline names the agent that wrote it (Claude �� Codex �� Qwen), and a card's `owner:` names who may work it. Never sign another agent's name, never edit another agent's card to describe your work, and never start a card whose `owner:` is not you �?" moving a card is the owner's action, and `owner: qwen` on a card is not an invitation for Claude or Claude's for Qwen. Observed 2026-10-12: a Qwen session ran Claude's cards and signed Claude's name on a note and a status file; the fix cost an owner correction, not a rollback.\n- **One board, one session at a time.** More than one agent session may be live on `D:\\Claude` (observed 2026-10-12: two Claude sessions committing to `archeus-v1-rearchitecture` within the same minutes). Before touching files, move the card to `doing` and fill `scope:` with every file it will touch, so the other session sees the claim; no two `doing` cards share a file. If a card's `commits:` already holds a SHA you did not write, stop �?" the work exists; verify it and record the verification instead of doing it again. Never commit the same card from two sessions.\n- **Canonical documents are appended to, never rewritten**: PV-20 design, ADRs, `p*-design-gate.md`, the S1 gate and acceptance. Records of built work go in as new gate items and dated \"as built\" amendments, under an authorized card; nothing already recorded is reworded. A material conflict between the code and a canonical document goes to the owner as a `BLOCKED BY OWNER` card.\n- **Evidence claims stay exact.** Report what ran, on what, with what result. A test not run is \"not run\".\n\n## Card format\n\n```\n### T-NNN �� <title>\nowner: claude | qwen �� status: proposed | ready | doing | review | changes | BLOCKED BY CLASSIFIER | BLOCKED BY OWNER | done\nauth: <owner's words, YYYY-MM-DD> | �?"\nscope: <files>\nrefs: <canonical docs or gate items this must satisfy>\naccept: <what done means, checkable>\nverify: <exact test command(s)>\ncommits: <sha, sha>\nreview: <reviews/T-NNN.md verdict>\nnotes: <blocks, decisions, open questions>\n```\n\nCards meant for Qwen must stand alone: name the files, functions, acceptance and test command. It does not share Claude's context.\n\n## Commands\n\nFrom `D:\\Claude`, in PowerShell or Git Bash:\n\n```\npy .collab\\review.py T-012            # Codex reviews T-012's commits, writes reviews\\T-012.md\npy .collab\\review.py --pending        # review every card in status review that has no verdict yet\npi --model spark/qwen3.8-flash-next \"Read .collab/README.md, then do card T-012 in .collab/BOARD.md.\"\npi -p --model spark/qwen3.8-flash-next \"<same prompt>\"   # headless; Claude launches Qwen this way\n```\n\nWhen Codex is out of quota, `review.py` exits non-zero and writes nothing. The card stays in `review` and the next `--pending` run picks it up. Implementation does not wait for review; it continues on the next `ready` card whose scope does not overlap. Observed 2026-10-12: a trivial `codex exec -s read-only` probe returned *\"You've hit your usage limit�?� try again at 1:29 PM\"* and no verdict was written �?" the reset time it names is the only reliable way to tell \"out of quota\" from \"broken\".\n\nCards with no commits �?" analysis cards, whose `scope:` is a `.collab` note �?" are skipped by `--pending`, because there is nothing for `git show` to read. They reach review only when the owner or Codex reads the file itself; `review: pending` on such a card means that, not a lost run.\n\n## Owner-authorized coordination update - 2026-10-12\n\n- Ordinary implementation, tests and routine docs belong to Claude. Qwen takes only owner-routed cards Claude cannot complete or is expected to be classifier-blocked on. Preventive assignment is now explicitly permitted by owner; label it \"preventive routing\", never invent an observed refusal.\n- Current preventive assignment is T014, analysis corrections only. Qwen has finished T006 verification; do not duplicate its implementation or full-suite run. No new Windows run follows from routing.\n- Codex may write .collab coordination/review docs under the owner's explicit request of this date. This exception authorizes no application edits, tests, canonical ADR changes, commits, or OS operations.\n- Direct reviews are stored with the same commit-keyed filenames as review.py and identify their provenance. Analysis reviews use T-NNN-analysis.md. Do not regenerate already-reviewed commits merely because review was produced directly rather than by the headless wrapper.\n- Never reproduce withheld content or treat model routing as removing authorization/security limits. A concrete permitted artifact can be assessed on its own merits.\n- T012 corrections and T014 analysis corrections use disjoint files. Planning T016/T017/T018 touches only each card's new note. T015 implementation remains proposed until its exact scope has owner authorization.\n- Update BOARD in one short serialized edit; reread before saving. Claim product files before changing them; no same-file concurrent edits, no second implementation of an existing commit.\n- Queue: Claude addresses T012 changes and ready planning T016/T017/T018; Qwen handles T014. T004/T008 stay blocked for the reasons on their cards.\n\n## Standing owner rule: parallel cards at every closeout - 2026-10-13\n\nOwner: \"Put everytime every card we can do in parallel to speed up archeus implementation as fast as possible\".\n\nCodex prepares every concrete useful independent next card current authority supports, not arbitrarily one card. Reuse existing cards/helpers, no duplicate work/speculative notes. Name owner, files, dependencies, acceptance and exact verification. Authorized code/planning ready; material choices/native actions blocked/proposed.\n\nClaude owns ordinary implementation/disjoint planning; sessions/forks claim disjoint files. Qwen only owner-routed actual or predicted classifier-blocked permitted work, prediction basis recorded, no invented refusal. Codex reviews/coordinates, no app edits/tests. Routing never bypasses policy.\n\nOne doing card per file per checkout. Separate reversible checkout for source work that would drift validation; frozen trees stay frozen. No merge/integration without review/authority. Hash-guard serialized board saves, preserve concurrent claims. Prefer focused checks and one combined regression over pointless broad repeats. Failed/aborted runs stay failure/no-result. Do not ask again for approved actions; keep missing material/native/provider/CLI/secret/cleanup authority explicit.\n## Review routing: Codex is for architecture only - 2026-10-14 (owner)\n\nOwner 2026-10-14 ~16:50 in the Claude session (verbatim): \"yes codex just has to make architectural decisions and review architecture integrity so don't give it everything at once\".\n\n- **Codex** reviews only architecture: decisions, contracts and invariants, canonical documents, cross-slice integration, and evidence that gates an architecture decision. It gets **one card at a time**, in the order on the board's review queue, never `--pending` over the whole queue. Its quota is the constraint.\n- **Implementation-only cards** (tests, CI, tooling, adapter flags, bug fixes inside an accepted design) are reviewed by an independent Claude reviewer: a fresh agent with no implementation context, read-only, same verdict format (`VERDICT: APPROVE|CHANGES|ESCALATE`), written to `reviews/T-NNN-<sha7>.claude.md` so it is never mistaken for a Codex file. The owner can send any of them to Codex.\n- A card's `review:` line says which reviewer gave the verdict.\n\n## Standing owner rule: Claude runs the queue; Codex reviews what matters; Qwen takes classifier work - 2026-10-15 (owner)\n\nOwner 2026-10-15 ~00:30 in the Claude session (verbatim): \"Ok, i give you permission as we go forward to continue inplementing even without codex review as codex usage is limited, we can let it review the important parts when it resets and is available, when it resets everytime just let it review the most important stuff you want, i want you to be the one to manage all 3 you, codex and qwen tasks, qwen tasks are those you and codex get blocked on because of classifier, so if you have doubt about an implementation or something you know will trigger safety classifier you can give it to qwen even before you reading them\"\n\n- **Implementation does not wait for review.** Claude continues on authorized cards. A card's commits may be built on before a verdict. The card stays `review` until a verdict arrives or the owner accepts it.\n- **Claude manages the queue for all three agents.** That covers which card goes next, who takes it, and what Codex sees at each reset. Codex gets the architecture-critical items first: contracts, invariants, canonical documents, cross-slice integration, evidence that gates a decision. Implementation-only cards go to the independent Claude reviewer.\n- **Claude routes to Qwen** any card on which Claude or Codex was stopped by a safety classifier. It may also route preventively, before reading the material, a card it expects to trigger the classifier. Routing is recorded on the card as \"preventive routing (owner rule 2026-10-15): <why expected>\", and no refusal is invented.\n- **Unchanged:** routing carries no authorization. Native Windows isolation, profile and ACL operations still need an `auth:` naming them. Push and merge-back of an integration candidate still need the owner. The hard rules above all hold.\n\n- **Claude does not read the content of Qwen's classifier-routed work** (owner 2026-10-15, verbatim: \"Do not read it's content if you know classifier will block it\"). That covers Qwen's note, the pi log and the diff. Claude checks only metadata: the exit code, the card's status line, whether the expected heading exists (`grep -c`) and that no tracked file changed. Codex reviews the content. Claude does not tail or print the pi log; it records the log path on the card for the owner.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792408763579}}
