{"type":"message","id":"c33ea96b","parentId":"d83fca10","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:22:20.343Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-b9c04146800c86b0","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## 7. Gate re-evaluation �?" O7 resolved (2026-10-08)\n\n**Owner decision received: O7 = UNCHARGED WITH BOUNDED BACKOFF/BREAKER.** Recorded verbatim:\n\n> - An isolation-establishment failure (`SpawnFailed`) is an infrastructure/isolation failure,\n>   not a failure of the user's task.\n> - It is therefore NOT charged against the task/execution outcome as task failure.\n> - Retry is permitted only under a bounded backoff policy.\n> - A breaker must prevent indefinite repeated attempts when isolation establishment is unhealthy.\n> - After the retry/breaker policy is exhausted, the condition is surfaced as an\n>   infrastructure/isolation failure requiring recovery/reconciliation.\n> - There is NEVER an unisolated fallback.\n> - There is NEVER a bypass of A4/isolation to make the execution run.\n> - The failure state must remain deterministically reconcilable by Core across restart.\n\n**7.1 �?" Sole blocker: satisfied.** The canonical mandate was a *decision*, delegated verbatim to\nP11/O7: PV-20 ��3.4 (\"*How an isolation-establishment failure is charged and whether it re-dispatches\n�?� PV-20 does not resolve O7; it requires P11's decision before implementation. Whatever O7 decides,\na spawn failure never bypasses isolation*\"), PV-20 ��3.7 (\"*charging is O7's*\"), PV-20 ��7.2 O7 row,\nADR-0053 ��1 and ��5 (R8). The decision resolves the delegated question. It preserves the canonical\ninvariants: no spawn on failure (��3.4/��6.3), and the no-bypass condition (\"*whatever O7 decides, a\nspawn failure never bypasses isolation*\") is restated inside the decision itself (\"NEVER an\nunisolated fallback\"; \"NEVER a bypass of A4/isolation\").\n\n**7.2 �?" No conflict with ��3.2 (A1).** \"*A structural workspace impossibility is rejected before\ndispatch and never enters a spawn/retry loop*\" binds **structural** refusals (still refused before\ndispatch); bounded retry applies only to **transient** isolation failures, and ��3.2/��3.4 defer\ncharging/re-dispatch *\"as P11's O7 decides\"* �?" \"uncharged\" is a legal value of that delegation.\n\n**7.3 �?" S1's nine-item scope UNCHANGED.** O7's demands on S1 �?" a `SpawnFailed`-class isolation-\nestablishment failure that is deterministically recorded, distinct from task failure, and\nreconcilable by Core across restart �?" fall inside existing items 3 (process-registry entry),\n6 (restart reconciliation) and 7 (isolated spawn path). The backoff/breaker **policy mechanism**\nconsumes those facts on the dispatch side (P11-owned, production dispatch E-gated) and is **not**\na new S1 component. *Re-gate trigger:* should the owner later assign the retry/breaker policy\nmechanism itself into S1, that is a scope change and re-enters this gate.\n\n**7.4 �?" Mandatory-language scan re-run** over PV-20 ��3.1, ��3.4, ��3.7, ��7.1�?"��7.5, ��8 and ADR-0053\n��1, ��4, ��5, ��7: the only \"required before implementation\" mandates were the three O7 citations,\nall now discharged. No new owner-mandated prerequisite for S1. Recorded nuances, none blocking:\n��3.7's restart \"resolve �?� through terminate / reap / fail\" is **record-level resolution** under\nR6-A �?" O7 forbids charging that resolution to the **task** as task failure; the two coexist.\nGap B / `record_end` remains H2/S7 (unresolved, non-blocking).\n\n**7.5 �?" Confirmations (items 1�?"10 of the re-evaluation request).**\n1. Sole blocker satisfied �?" ��7.1. 2. Nine-item scope unaltered �?" ��7.3. 3. No new mandatory\nprerequisite �?" ��7.4. 4. Mandatory-language scan re-run �?" ��7.4; no other S1 blocker found.\n5. Spike evidence remains valid (O7 is Core/dispatch semantics; it touches no OS-boundary finding;\n9/9 stands). 6. A7 remains: `PAUSING` at Core restart ��' `PAUSED`; SpawnFailed is a *never-spawned*\nclass, outside A7's exit classification �?" no interaction, no dilution. 7. H2 scope (`record_end`\nevery-vs-isolated) remains UNRESOLVED and non-blocking for S1. 8. E ordering remains UNRESOLVED and\nnon-blocking for S1. 9. A4 remains BUILT-but-NOT-ENFORCED in S1; production enforcement belongs to\nE �?" O7's \"never bypass A4\" forbids any convenience bypass, it does not require enforcement inside\nS1, where no production dispatch exists to bypass. 10. **No permission to modify production\ndispatch**: the retry/breaker policy operates only where dispatch already exists; S1 dispatches\nnothing, production dispatch remains unauthorized, and enabling it stays E's.\n\nAll spike-derived constraints of ��5 are preserved unchanged.\n\n---\n\n## S1 STATUS: **READY FOR AUTHORIZATION**\n\n*(supersedes BLOCKED 2026-10-08; sole blocker P11 O7 resolved by owner decision �?" ��7)*\n\n- **O7: SATISFIED** �?" UNCHARGED WITH BOUNDED BACKOFF/BREAKER (��7), consistent with PV-20 ��3.2/��3.4/��3.7\n  and ADR-0053 ��1/��5; canonical no-bypass invariant restated and preserved.\n- **Mandatory-language scan: no other canonical-mandatory S1 blocker remains** (��7.4).\n- All ��1 preconditions stand on evidence; ��4 scope is the authorized scope definition; ��5\n  constraints carry forward; ��3 later-slice classifications unchanged.\n- **THIS READY STATUS IS NOT AUTHORIZATION TO IMPLEMENT S1.** It records only that the owner may\n  now issue a separate explicit S1 implementation authorization. Until that authorization exists\n  verbatim: no source changes, no S1 implementation tests, no P20/ADR or source modification,\n  no commits, no push, no production dispatch, no A4 enforcement, no E implementation.\n- Any scope addition beyond the nine items of ��4, or any reassignment of the backoff/breaker\n  policy mechanism into S1, re-enters this gate.\n\n*STOP. Record only. Awaiting a separate, explicit S1 implementation authorization.*\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792408940343}}
{"type":"message","id":"f76fe44d","parentId":"c33ea96b","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:22:33.547Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the O7 decision recorded. Now I need to find:\n\n1. Where O7 is recorded and its exact words �?" PV20-S1-GATE ��7 (with the verbatim bullets), plus the origin p20-design-gate ��4.2 O7 row (line 371) and ��12 item 72 (line 1420).\n\n2. Whether the design names the breaker's location, threshold, window, reset and visibility, or leaves any open �?" each open one is an owner question. The O7 decision itself does NOT name threshold/window/reset/visibility. ��7.3 says the backoff/breaker policy mechanism is on the dispatch side (P11-owned, production dispatch E-gated), not a new S1 component. So the location is: dispatch side, P11-owned. But thresholds/window/reset/visibility are not named ��' open owner questions.\n\n3. Which existing breaker code could carry it: execution breaker AVAILABLE/DEGRADED/OPEN, pairing breaker, loop guard �?" with file:line. These are in the repo D:/archeus-p20-mcp at b73f875. Need to find file:line via `git show b73f875:<path>`.\n\n4. The dispatch path a permanent spawn failure takes today (file:line) and why it loops.\n\nNote the verify says: read-only grep and reading of D:/archeus-p20-mcp (git show b73f875:<path>); no code, no tests, no process, no native or ACL operation, no web fetch.\n\nImportant: the review file mentions p20-design-gate ��1.6 line 131-132: the execution breaker (`executions.py:675-705`, `stop_reason='breaker'`), the pairing breaker (`PairingLocked`, ...). Let me read that section for context, then find the actual code in the repo at b73f875.\n\nLet me look at p20-design-gate ��1.6 and the review file.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd6c9dc242d31f58","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":20,"offset":128,"path":"D:/Claude/docs/architecture/p20-design-gate.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-85816c7e9303662e","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"D:/Claude/.collab/reviews/T-102-c91d4f1.claude.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"spark","model":"qwen3.8-flash-next","usage":{"input":2916,"output":565,"cacheRead":23296,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":453,"totalTokens":26777,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792408940344,"responseId":"chatcmpl-b243552ca6ee1ef6","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"788434c5","parentId":"f76fe44d","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:22:33.551Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-bd6c9dc242d31f58","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"  at the start of slice F, not assumed. **Codex is not installed.**\r\n\r\n### 1.6 Breakers, secrets, dependencies [V]\r\n- Breakers that exist: the execution breaker (per account, resource/auth failures,\r\n  `executions.py:675-705`, `stop_reason = 'breaker'`), the pairing breaker (`PairingLocked`,\r\n  `api/auth.py:208`), five wrong PINs revoking a device (`routes.py:844`), and P14's automation\r\n  loop guard (S10b).\r\n- Secrets Archeus holds at rest: `run/local-token` (POSIX `0600`; on Windows the profile ACL,\r\n  p3.5b D5, DPAPI deferred by the user); device, node and hook tokens are stored hashed. No API\r\n  key is stored by Core (`AUTH_KINDS` includes `api_key`, but accounts are node-bound and Core\r\n  keeps metadata). This is verified in slice I, not assumed.\r\n- Python runtime: stdlib only. SPA: `clients/app/package-lock.json`. Neither has had an audit\r\n  recorded. No fuzzing or property-testing library is installed (`hypothesis` is absent).\r\n\r\n### 1.7 Phase markers [V]\r\nTwo `xfail(strict, reason=\"phase:P20\")`: G5's Core-less e-stop, and G7's Codex adapter\r\n(`tests/v1/contract/test_adapter_contract.py:229`, p11 D30).\r\n\r\n## 2. Contradictions this gate cannot resolve alone\r\n\r\n\n[2776 more lines in file. Use offset=148 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792408953551}}
{"type":"message","id":"1f32b758","parentId":"788434c5","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:22:33.551Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-85816c7e9303662e","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: CHANGES\n\nReview of T-102, commit c91d4f1 (detached checkout D:/archeus-p20-mcp, HEAD c91d4f1, on c1cc825). I did not edit product code. Part (1), the orphan kill, is sound and its tests are real. Part (2), routing an OSError from spawn through `_refuse`, swaps a fail-stop crash for a re-dispatch loop with no bound. It also contradicts p11-design-gate note 11. That is the one blocking finding.\n\nFindings:\n1. archeus/harnesses/base.py:323-327 with archeus/core/execution/manager.py:323-330. Blocking.\n   Problem: an OSError raised before any child exists now becomes SpawnFailed, and `_spawn` refuses it as `binding`, which is uncharged. The task goes back to READY and is dispatched again, and nothing bounds that. When the failure persists (Core's exec dir not writable, disk full, a path too long under ARCHEUS_HOME, an AV lock), it is re-dispatched on every pass, forever.\n   Measured on a git-archive copy of c91d4f1 with a scratch test (deleted afterwards). With the prompt write failing permanently, 4 s of `engine.step` + `manager.tick` produced 70 executions, all ABANDONED, stop_reason `binding`, charged False. The task was still READY with max_attempts 2. In the runtime the loop is tight: `tick()` returns truthy, so ExecLoop never takes its EXEC_S idle wait. Each pass writes an Execution row and its events, an exec dir holding a marker and a tombstone, and, if the MCP opt-in is on, an MCP config holding the opted-in keys, which is written and then dropped.\n   Before this commit the same persistent failure raised out of `tick()`. ExecLoop's `except Exception` then stopped Core (exit 3). On restart, `_reconcile_one` read the marker with no pid.json as LOST, which is charged, so the failure stopped after max_attempts. This change turns a loud failure that stopped into a silent one that never stops.\n   Canonical conflict: p11-design-gate note 11 (D9) says \"`binding` again means only a stale plan binding (��7) or provider terms\". It made a workspace that cannot be made a **charged** `stop_reason='workspace'`, because the uncharged version looped 395 times in one run. The manager states the same rule at manager.py:288-293 for the settings inventory: \"an uncharged end re-dispatches the task, so a failure that persists would start a new execution on every tick, forever\". `_workspace` already charges an OSError from `os.makedirs(ExecPaths(e.id).work)`, which is the same tree base.spawn creates. The owner's O7 decision (PV20-S1-GATE ��7, 2026-10-08) makes only an *isolation-establishment* SpawnFailed uncharged, and even that only \"under a bounded backoff policy\" with a breaker. No such mechanism exists yet. A prompt or marker write failure is not isolation establishment.\n   Required change: keep the refusal and the tombstone, but charge a pre-child IO refusal. One way: base.spawn raises a SpawnFailed subclass (or a tagged SpawnFailed) for the OSError branch, and `_spawn` refuses it with `stop_reason='workspace'`. `binding` stays for isolation and O7. Then add a test in which the failure persists and the task ends FAILED after max_attempts, alongside the existing one-shot test (which should still reach ENDED_OK on the second attempt with max_attempts 2). If the implementer wants this uncharged, that extends O7 beyond its decided scope and needs a breaker. It is an owner or Codex decision, so the card should then go BLOCKED BY OWNER and not merge as is.\n   Related, pre-existing and not introduced here: a Popen failure (`spawn_detached` returning `(None, err)`, then `_refuse`, then `binding`) loops the same way. A scratch probe gave 63 uncharged ABANDONED executions in 4 s. That is the open O7 breaker work. It needs its own card if it is not folded into the fix above.\n2. archeus/core/execution/manager.py:142-167 and boot() at :760-788. Non-blocking, follow-up card.\n   Problem: first sight lives only in the in-memory `_procs` entry. It holds across ticks, but nothing survives a Core restart. `boot()` reconciles only non-terminal executions, so a terminal execution's process that is still running (exactly T-102's case) is never adopted and never killed by Core after a restart. `base.sweep_mcp_configs(set(self._procs))` also removes its MCP config while the process still runs. The only thing that reaches it is the Core-less e-stop (`LocalNode.estop_without_core` through the registry's unended lines, called only from cli/main.py:361). A possible follow-up: at boot, kill (by identity) every unended registry spawn whose execution is terminal.\n3. claude_sessions/proc.py:257-288 (pre-existing, shared with `_control`'s STOPPING kill). Non-blocking.\n   Is it a tree kill? On Windows, yes: `taskkill /F /T` is forced, and /T walks parent-pid links, so a grandchild whose parent already exited is missed. That is acknowledged by the ponytail comment, and a Job is the real fix. On POSIX the kill is SIGTERM (15) to the process group, not SIGKILL, so a process that \"ignores the stop\" may ignore that too. When a kill does not take, `_orphans` re-issues it on every pass with `did += 1`, so the exec thread spins without idling (on Windows it spawns a taskkill subprocess per pass). A Windows taskkill that partly fails while the root lives makes `adapter.stop` raise StopRefused, which says \"not the process this execution started\", and `node.kill` then returns 'gone'. The STOPPING path already behaves the same way, so the commit does not make it worse. Worth a note on the card: \"tree kill\" holds fully on Windows only.\n4. tests/v1/integration/test_settings_sources.py:391-450. Nit.\n   The process that \"ignores the stop\" is simulated by patching `adapter.status` to report 'running'. When the kill is issued, the real fake child has halted and usually exited, so `kill_pid_tree` is a no-op on a pid that is gone. The tests prove that `node.kill` is called at the right moment and that the next pass collects the process. They do not show that a live process tree dies. That is acceptable, because the tree kill is node.kill's existing behaviour, but the card's \"a test with a fake process that ignores the stop\" overstates it slightly. Separately, my mutant widening the refusal to every non-KeyboardInterrupt exception (M6 below) survived, so nothing pins the OSError-only scope.\n5. Commit message. Wording nit.\n   It says the OSError \"aborted the whole tick\". It aborted the exec thread: ExecLoop logs \"the execution thread failed; Core stops (exit 3)\". This is relevant to finding 1, since the old behaviour stopped and the new one does not.\n\nQuestions asked:\n- Is first sight persisted across ticks? Yes: `p['since']` is in the `_procs` entry. The realistic way in is AWAITING_APPROVAL with the halt still exiting, then a user stop, e-stop or account ceiling discards it. In that state `_watch` ��' `_control` resets `since` to None on every pass, so the grace starts at first orphan sight. A `since` inherited from a STOPPING, PAUSING or HANDING_OFF timer could only start the grace earlier, never later. I found no path where one of those states turns terminal while its process is still in `_procs` and has not been collected. Across a restart, see finding 2.\n- Could it kill a process that is still needed? I found none.\n  - Hand-off or PreCompact: `_collect` pops the old process before `record_handoff` ends that execution, and the continuation gets a new execution id.\n  - Resume: it reuses the id, but only from PAUSED or AWAITING_APPROVAL, which are live and therefore outside the orphan pass. `_step` sends an AWAITING_APPROVAL execution whose process is still in `_procs` to `_watch`, never to `_resume`, so a resume never overwrites a tracked process.\n  - New attempt: a new execution id. `_procs` is keyed by id.\n  - PID reuse: `node.kill` checks (pid, create_time) first. The window between the check and the kill is acknowledged in proc.py.\n- Double kill or collect race: `_orphans` takes only ids outside this tick's live snapshot, and `_collect` pops the entry. An execution ended by a route thread during a tick goes through `_watch` on that tick and `_orphans` on the next, so it can take a second, idempotent kill. That is harmless. A collected terminal execution returns before `record_end` (`_collect` reloads it, sees it ended, and only releases isolation).\n- Is `binding` the right failure class with a retry bound? No, and there is no bound. See finding 1.\n\nRan:\n- `git -C D:/archeus-p20-mcp archive c91d4f1`, extracted to D:/trv-T102-src. `py -m pytest tests/v1/integration/test_settings_sources.py tests/v1/integration/test_execution.py tests/v1/integration/test_estop_drills.py -m \"not real_process\" -p no:cacheprovider --basetemp=D:/trv-T102`: 96 passed.\n- The same copy with `-k \"T099 or T102\"` on test_settings_sources.py: 14 passed, which matches the card.\n- Mutants on the copy, against the T099+T102 tests, each restored byte for byte:\n  - M1 (no kill): CAUGHT, 2 failed.\n  - M2 (grace ignored): CAUGHT, 2 failed.\n  - M3 (e-stop ignored): CAUGHT, 1 failed.\n  - M4 (OSError not refused): CAUGHT, 1 failed.\n  - M5 (first sight re-taken on every tick): CAUGHT, 1 failed.\n  - M7 (grace counted from 0, so a kill on first sight): CAUGHT, 2 failed.\n  - M6 (refuse any exception except KeyboardInterrupt): SURVIVED, 14 passed.\n  - After restoring: 14 passed. The card's four mutants are confirmed.\n- Scratch probes on the copy, with a 4 s engine+manager loop: a permanent prompt-write failure gave 70 uncharged `binding` ABANDONED executions with the task still READY. A permanent Popen failure gave 63 (pre-existing).\n- `py -m ruff check` on manager.py, base.py and test_settings_sources.py: all checks passed. `git show --check c91d4f1`: clean.\n- D:/trv-T102 and D:/trv-T102-src deleted. D:/archeus-p20-mcp `git status` is clean at c91d4f1.\n\nNot checked: the card's 966-test focused set (not rerun); the full tests/v1 suite; POSIX (no SIGTERM-ignoring process was tried); the real CLI; the isolated path (read only: an OSError from `authority.establish` now also becomes a SpawnFailed, then `note_failure`, then `binding`, which is latent while isolated dispatch is off); a real process that ignores the stop; a Core restart while an orphan is running.\n\nReviewer: independent Claude agent (general-purpose), 2026-10-15\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792408953551}}
